(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-arterial-occlusive-aneurysm-vignettes"]={"title":"Arterial Occlusive Disease and Aortic Aneurysm Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 69-year-old man has sudden severe pain in his right leg. Pulses are absent below the knee. Which examination finding is often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency?","choices":["Sensory loss over the foot's dorsum","Complete paralysis of the leg","Absent patellar reflex","Loss of hair on the shin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His sudden severe pain with absent pulses below the knee is acute limb ischemia. Light-touch sensory fibers are highly susceptible to ischemia, so sensory deficits over the dorsum of the foot are often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency, which is why sensory testing is part of the examination. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has a 3.1 cm femoral pseudoaneurysm that has persisted since his catheterization. Which treatment seals the leak?","choices":["Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Ultrasound-guided thrombin injection","Watch and wait","Intravenous heparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His pseudoaneurysm is 3.1 cm, larger than the under-2 cm size that may thrombose and regress spontaneously, and it has persisted. Ultrasound-guided injection of thrombin, which promotes clot, seals the leak. Watch and wait is reserved for those under 2 cm. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with sudden leg ischemia has sensory loss extending beyond the toes with rest pain, mild-to-moderate weakness, an inaudible arterial Doppler signal and an audible venous signal. Where should his evaluation and treatment take place?","choices":["Nowhere; plan major amputation","In the surgical suite, without delay","After outpatient duplex imaging","After computed tomography first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sensory loss beyond the toes with rest pain, mild-to-moderate weakness, an inaudible arterial Doppler signal and an audible venous signal define an immediately threatened limb. It is salvageable with immediate revascularization, so it should preferentially undergo further evaluation and treatment in a surgical suite. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with hypertension and an acute dissection has had his systolic pressure lowered to 110 mmHg and his pain controlled. Which definitive treatment removes the diseased segment?","choices":["Surgical grafting of the aorta","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Ultrasound-guided thrombin","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With his systolic pressure at 110 mmHg, inside the 100 to 120 mmHg target, and his pain controlled, the definitive step is surgery: grafting and replacing the diseased portion of the aorta. Blood pressure reduction stops progression, but only the graft removes the diseased segment. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a 4.8 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm has a history of severe reaction to contrast dye. She is stable, and the extent of the aneurysm must be defined. Which imaging is most appropriate?","choices":["Conventional angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Computed tomography angiography","Abdominal X-ray"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance imaging is comparable to computed tomography without exposure to dye or ionizing radiation, which makes it useful in stable patients with a dye allergy. She is stable, her severe contrast reaction rules out dye-based studies, and the extent of her 4.8 cm aneurysm can still be defined. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has an incidental 4.0 cm ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm on a chest radiograph and follow-up imaging. He has no symptoms. Which surveillance plan is appropriate?","choices":["Abdominal ultrasound every three years with no other imaging","Open repair with aortic root replacement within the month","Computed tomography or magnetic resonance every 3 to 5 years","Computed tomography or magnetic resonance every 6 to 12 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A root, ascending or descending thoracic aneurysm under 4.5 cm is followed with computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging every 3 to 5 years. His 4.0 cm ascending aneurysm is asymptomatic and below every repair trigger, so this interval applies. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension reports calf pain after walking about two blocks that resolves with rest. His ankle-brachial index is 0.62 on the left and normal on the right. Which interpretation fits the left-sided value?","choices":["Rest pain and tissue loss range","Noncompressible artery range","Claudication range","Normal range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An ankle-brachial index of 0.4 to 0.9 is the claudication range, and his left value of 0.62 sits within it. It matches his calf pain after walking two blocks that resolves with rest, while his normal right side lies above 0.9 up to 1.3. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had a cold, pulseless right leg for three hours, with pallor, tingling and mild weakness of the toes. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Immediate vascular surgery consult","Outpatient ankle-brachial index","Supervised exercise referral","Start cilostazol and follow up"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A cold, pulseless leg for three hours with pallor, tingling and toe weakness is acute limb ischemia, a vascular emergency, and weakness places it at least in the immediately threatened category. Management begins with an immediate vascular surgery consult and intravenous heparin, with testing guided by the category of ischemia. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man who smokes a pack a day has calf claudication and an ankle-brachial index of 0.7. He asks what matters most for his legs. Which counseling point is most important?","choices":["Stop smoking completely","Limit walking to avoid pain","Elevate the legs when painful","Switch to occasional cigars"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cigarette smoking carries three times the risk of peripheral artery disease compared with nonsmokers, and smoking cessation leads the list of risk-factor reduction measures in medical management. For a pack-a-day smoker with claudication and an index of 0.7, quitting completely matters most. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 11, 21, 50"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has pain in both legs when walking, with numbness and weakness. Standing still does not help; the pain eases only when he sits down or leans forward over a shopping cart. His pedal pulses are normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Spinal stenosis","Peripheral artery disease","Peripheral neuropathy","Venous insufficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Walking-induced pain in both legs with numbness and weakness, not relieved by standing still but eased by sitting or leaning forward over a cart, is spinal stenosis. It is relieved by rest and by sitting down or leaning forward to straighten out the lumbar spine, and his normal pedal pulses argue against arterial disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 19, 34, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension has sudden, severe, ripping chest pain. His electrocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Computed tomography, chest and abdomen","Reassure him; the tracing is normal","Ankle-brachial index measurement","Abdominal plain radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sudden, severe, ripping chest pain in a man with hypertension is the presentation of aortic dissection, and the electrocardiogram may be normal in dissection, so his normal tracing does not exclude it. Computed tomography of the chest and abdomen delineates the extent of the dissection. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who smokes and has diabetes reports aching in both buttocks and hips after walking two blocks, relieved by about 10 minutes of rest. He also reports erectile dysfunction. Both femoral pulses are diminished. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tibial artery disease","Aortoiliac occlusive disease","Superficial femoral artery disease","Lumbar spinal stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Buttock and hip claudication points to aortoiliac disease, which can diminish one or both femoral pulses and, when severe, causes erectile dysfunction. Claudication, absent or diminished femoral pulses and erectile dysfunction together form the Leriche syndrome triad, and he has all three. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man presents with painful blue toes and palpable pedal pulses. On abdominal examination there is a pulsatile mass above the umbilicus. Which lesion is the likely source of his emboli?","choices":["Carotid artery plaque","Femoral pseudoaneurysm","Thoracic aortic dissection","Abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm can produce embolic phenomena that lead to blue toe syndrome. His pulsatile mass above the umbilicus is the aneurysm, and painful blue toes with palpable pedal pulses fit small emboli lodging in the digits rather than a large-artery occlusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has a suddenly cool left foot with mild toe pain. She has no sensory loss and no muscle weakness, and venous Doppler signals are present. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Immediate major amputation","Direct transfer to the surgical suite","Discharge with oral aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A suddenly cool foot with mild toe pain, no sensory loss, no weakness and audible venous Doppler signals is a viable or marginally threatened limb. Both categories have sufficient time for vascular imaging such as computed tomography angiography before treatment, which makes it the next step here. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a 5.9 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm is being prepared for repair. Which study is best for diagnosis and pre-intervention planning?","choices":["Abdominal X-ray","Transesophageal echocardiography","Computed tomography angiography","Abdominal ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography is the best diagnostic and pre-intervention planning study, accurately delineating the location, size and extent of the aneurysm. For a 5.9 cm aneurysm already being prepared for repair, that anatomical detail is what the surgeon needs. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with extensive atherosclerosis develops painful, tender, bluish discoloration of several toes on his left foot, with localized pallor. His dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are easily palpable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute embolic limb ischemia","Critical limb ischemia","Dry gangrene","Atheroembolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atheroemboli of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris from proximal plaques lodge in small vessels of muscle and skin, causing blue toe syndrome with localized tenderness, pallor and livedo reticularis. Because large vessels may not be occluded, distal pulses usually remain palpable, as his are. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has constant hypogastric and low back pain for two days. A tender pulsatile mass is felt; blood pressure is 152/90 and stable. Imaging shows a 5.8 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm without rupture. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge with smoking advice","Refer once it reaches 6.5 cm","Beta blocker and surgical repair","Ultrasound again in six months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two days of hypogastric and low back pain with a tender pulsatile mass and stable pressure is a symptomatic, unruptured aneurysm. Management is to evaluate hemodynamic stability, place large-bore intravenous catheters, control pain, control blood pressure with beta blockers and perform surgical repair unless contraindicated. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has constant throbbing, burning pain in her toes and forefoot that is worse lying flat, not relieved by narcotic pain medicine, and eased by dangling her leg or walking around. Her feet are cool. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Peripheral neuropathy","Acute limb ischemia","Critical limb ischemia","Intermittent claudication"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain of critical limb ischemia is constant, throbbing or burning, in the forefoot and toes, with cool feet. It worsens with elevation or reclining, is not readily controlled even by narcotics, and is relieved by dependency or walking around, which use gravity to aid perfusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 19, 21, 23, 40, 59"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with hypertension has a carotid bruit found on routine examination. She has never had neurologic symptoms. Duplex shows 35% stenosis. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Digital subtraction angiography","Medical management","Carotid endarterectomy","Carotid stenting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medical management is advised for symptomatic and asymptomatic patients with low-grade stenosis under 50%, and hers is 35% without symptoms. It includes antiplatelet therapy such as aspirin or clopidogrel, control of hypertension, diabetes and lipids, smoking cessation, healthy diet, exercise and weight reduction. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man arrives with a suddenly cold, pale, pulseless left foot. Vascular surgery has been called. Which medical therapy should be started?","choices":["Cilostazol","Pentoxifylline","Intravenous heparin","Oral aspirin alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A suddenly cold, pale, pulseless foot is acute peripheral arterial occlusion, and its medical therapy is anticoagulation with intravenous heparin, started alongside the immediate vascular surgery consult. Diagnostic testing then depends on the category of ischemia. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with a femoral pseudoaneurysm after catheterization reports increasing thigh pain and tingling around the swelling. Which explains her pain?","choices":["Retroperitoneal rupture of the aorta","Critical limb ischemia rest pain","Atheroemboli to the toes","Swelling and nerve compression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm presents with pain due to increased pressure from swelling or from nerve compression. Her increasing thigh pain with tingling around the swelling after femoral catheterization matches both mechanisms, the tingling pointing to a compressed nerve. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man who smokes and has hypertension had a transient ischemic attack four months ago: weakness of his left arm that fully cleared within an hour. A right carotid bruit is heard, and duplex ultrasonography shows significant stenosis. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Asymptomatic carotid artery disease","Thoracic aortic aneurysm","Aortic dissection","Symptomatic carotid artery disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A transient ischemic attack (a focal loss of neurologic function lasting less than 24 hours) within the last six months makes carotid disease symptomatic. His left arm weakness cleared within an hour four months ago, and he has a right carotid bruit and significant stenosis on duplex. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman being treated for an acute aortic dissection develops a new diastolic murmur and worsening breathlessness. Which complication explains these findings?","choices":["Aortic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Pseudoaneurysm","Carotid stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection can cause aortic regurgitation through dilatation of the aortic root, alongside its other presenting features of tearing chest pain, syncope and hypertension or hypotension. A new diastolic murmur with worsening breathlessness during treatment for a dissection fits that complication. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with claudication and an ankle-brachial index of 0.72 has no bleeding history. Which medication class should be started to lower his risk of myocardial infarction and stroke?","choices":["Intravenous heparin","A loop diuretic","An antiplatelet agent","A thrombolytic agent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin, clopidogrel or cilostazol decreases the overall risk of cardiovascular disease from myocardial infarction and stroke and improves walking distance by enhancing circulation. With claudication, an index of 0.72 and no bleeding history, he is a clear candidate. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with a known 4.2 cm thoracic aortic aneurysm comes to urgent care with new chest and back pain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Routine imaging in six months","Reassurance and pain relief","Ankle-brachial index today","Urgent imaging of the aorta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chest or back pain with a known or suspected thoracic aortic aneurysm must undergo urgent imaging to rule out the aneurysm as the cause. Her new pain with a known 4.2 cm aneurysm fits exactly, and if the aneurysm is the cause, repair is recommended for all symptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysms. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man who had a large anterior myocardial infarction three weeks ago develops sudden pain and coldness of his right leg with absent foot pulses. Which cardiac finding is the most likely embolic source?","choices":["A calcified mitral leaflet","A pericardial effusion","An atrial septal scar","Left ventricular thrombus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thromboemboli are the most common emboli in acute limb ischemia, and the heart is the source in 80 to 90% of cases, for example atrial fibrillation or a left ventricular thrombus after a myocardial infarction. His large anterior infarction three weeks ago makes a ventricular thrombus the likely source. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with carotid artery disease on duplex smokes a pack a day and asks whether quitting still matters now that he takes aspirin. Which counseling point is accurate?","choices":["Smoking harms only the leg arteries","Smoking nearly doubles stroke risk","Aspirin cancels the smoking risk","Quitting only matters before surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoking nearly doubles the risk of stroke, so smoking cessation is part of the medical management of carotid artery disease. It sits alongside antiplatelet therapy such as his aspirin, control of hypertension, diabetes and lipids, healthy diet, exercise and weight reduction. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with claudication, a known contrast allergy and reduced renal function needs arterial imaging before a planned intervention. Which study is relatively contraindicated for her?","choices":["Ankle-brachial index","Computed tomography angiography","Magnetic resonance angiography","Duplex ultrasound"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography uses contrast to delineate arterial anatomy, and its listed contraindications are contrast allergy and decreased renal function. She has both, so it is the study to avoid; magnetic resonance angiography is the alternative in contrast allergy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with peripheral artery disease has a hard, dry, black distal toe with a clear line between healthy and dead tissue. There is no swelling, moisture or fever. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atheroembolism","Dry gangrene","Wet gangrene","Gas gangrene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dry gangrene has a hard, dry texture, usually involves the distal toes, shows clear demarcation between healthy and necrotic tissue, and is common in patients with peripheral artery disease. His toe matches every feature, with no swelling, moisture or fever to suggest infection. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman had a 20-minute episode of right arm weakness and slurred speech that fully resolved. A left carotid bruit is heard. Which is the best initial test for her carotid arteries?","choices":["Digital subtraction angiography","Computed tomography of the chest","Ankle-brachial index","Duplex ultrasonography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her 20-minute episode of right arm weakness and slurred speech that fully resolved, with a left carotid bruit, points to carotid disease. Duplex ultrasonography is the most widely used initial screening tool and is also used to monitor patients serially for progression or after intervention. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 74"}]};
